`r\'[-\\u2013\\u2014]\'` to find dash characters, but misses mathematical minus 
sign `−` (U+2212) and figure dash `‒` (U+2012). When `lines_read` contains these
characters (e.g., "10−20" copied from formatted text), the method fails to parse
the range and returns empty list, causing `should_read_file` to report "Cannot 
determine - invalid lines_read format" even though the format is valid.\n\n## 
Lens\nPhase 1: The Intent vs. Reality Check (Logic Gaps)\n\n## Expected vs. 
Observed\n| Expected Behavior | Observed Risk |\n|---|---|\n| All common 
dash-like characters should be recognized: hyphen `-`, en dash `–`, em dash `—`,
minus sign `−`, figure dash `‒`. | Only hyphen, en dash, and em dash are 
recognized. |\n| "10−20" (with mathematical minus) should parse as 
LineRange(10,20). | Returns empty list, treated as invalid format. |\n| 
Consistent parsing regardless of which dash character is used. | Inconsistent: 
depends on character choice. |\n\n## Root Cause\nThe regex pattern 
`r\'[-\\u2013\\u2014]\'` in `_parse_line_ranges` explicitly lists only three 
characters:\n- Hyphen `-` (ASCII 45)\n- En dash `–` (U+2013)  \n- Em dash `—` 
(U+2014)\n\nMissing:\n- Figure dash `‒` (U+2012)\n- Minus sign `−` (U+2212)\n- 
Horizontal bar `―` (U+2015)\n- Other dash-like Unicode characters\n\nWhen the 
regex doesn\'t match, the code treats the part as a single line number, tries 
`int("10−20")` which raises `ValueError`, catches it, and continues (skipping 
the range).\n\n## Fix Plan\n1. Expand regex to include common dash characters: 
`r\'[-\\u2012\\u2013\\u2014\\u2015\\u2212]\'`\n   - `\\u2012`: FIGURE DASH\n   -
`\\u2013`: EN DASH  \n   - `\\u2014`: EM DASH\n   - `\\u2015`: HORIZONTAL BAR\n 
- `\\u2212`: MINUS SIGN\n2. Alternatively, use Unicode property `\\p{Pd}` 
(Dash_Punctuation) but Python\'s `re` doesn\'t support Unicode properties by 
default.\n3. Keep backward compatibility with existing dash characters.\n\n## 
Status: IN PROGRESS\n\n## Files Changed\n(To be filled after fix)'})
